<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Seha River Land</h1><p class="st-meta">1320 BCE – 1180 BCE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Seha River Land, a Hittite vassal kingdom of western Anatolia, caught between the Hittites and the Mycenaean Greeks.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Seha River Land formed part of the old <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/arzawa">Arzawa</a> coalition in the valley of a western Anatolian river, probably the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakircay">Bakırçay</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gediz-river">Gediz</a>. After the fall of Arzawa c. 1320 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mursili-ii">Mursili II</a> confirmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manapa-tarhunta">Manapa-Tarhunta</a> as its king under a Hittite treaty.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its rulers governed the river valley and, at times, the nearby island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesbos">Lazpa</a>, probably Lesbos. A letter from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manapa-tarhunta">Manapa-Tarhunta</a> reports on events in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/wilusa">Wilusa</a>, and King Masturi married a sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muwatalli-ii">Muwatalli II</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tudhaliya-iv">Tudhaliya IV</a> a king backed by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ahhiyawa">Ahhiyawa</a> rebelled and was defeated. The kingdom fades from the record with the collapse of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a> c. 1180 BCE.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/seha-river-land.jpg" alt="Seha River Land" width="320" height="197"><figcaption>Image: Al-qamar · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hethiter.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1320 BCE – 1180 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century-bce">14th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century-bce">12th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Manapa-Tarhunta, Masturi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Luwian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Luwian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Seha</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-1250&amp;focus=seha-river-land" aria-label="Seha River Land on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5359 774 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Seha River Land lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5679 1030L5852 1002"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5852 1002h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5679 1030h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5679 1030h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-1250&amp;focus=seha-river-land">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
